Analysis of PDEs.summary:
In this paper, we investigate dead-core problems for fully nonlinear degenerate parabolic equations with strong absorption, \begin{equation*}
  |Du|^{p} F(D^{2}u) - u_{t} = λ_{0}(x,t)\, u^μ\, χ_{\{u>0\}}(x,t)
  \qquad \text{in } \quad Ω_{T} := Ω\times (0,T), \end{equation*} where $0\le p<\infty$, $0<μ<1$ and $u \geq 0 $ is a bounded viscosity solution satisfying $0\le u_t\le A_0u^μ$ in the viscosity sense for some $0\le A_0<\infty$. We establish a sharp and improved parabolic $C^α$-regularity estimate along the free boundary $\partial \{ u > 0 \}$, where \[ α:= \frac{2+p}{1+p-μ} > 1 + \frac{1}{1+p}. \] Moreover, we establish weak geometric properties of solutions, such as non-degeneracy and uniform positive density. As an application, we obtain a Liouville-type theorem for entire solutions and gradient bounds. Finally, as a byproduct of our approach, we derive a new formulation of the gradient decay property and establish a Harnack-type inequality on time slices. It is worth noting that the results presented here extend those in da Silva {\em et al.}(Pacific J. Math., \textbf{300} (2019), 179--213) and (J. Differential Equations., \textbf{264} (2018), 7270--7293) to the degenerate setting, and can be viewed as a parabolic analogue of da Silva {\em et al.} (Math. Nachr., \textbf{294} (2021), 38--55) and Teixeira (Math. Ann., \textbf{364} (2016), 1121--1134).

We consider the weakly coupled elliptic system of logistic type, \begin{equation}
  \begin{cases}
  -Δu &=μ_1u- |u|^{p-2}u+ β|u|^{\frac{p}{2}-2}u |v|{^{\frac{p}{2}}}\mbox{ in }Ω,
  -Δv & =μ_2 v- |v|^{p-2}v+β|u|^{\frac{p}{2}}|v|^{\frac{p}{2}-2}v \mbox{ in }Ω,
  \ \ u,v &\in H_0^1(Ω),
  \end{cases} \end{equation}
  where $Ω\subset\mathbb{R}^N$ is a bounded connected domain with $N\geq 2$, $2< p < 2^*$, $λ_1(Ω)< μ_1\leqμ_2$, and $λ_1(Ω)$ denotes the first eigenvalue of the Laplacian operator in $Ω$ with Dirichlet boundary conditions. We say the system is competitive if $β<0$ and cooperative if $β>0$, for $β\in \mathbb{R}$.
  We prove the existence and multiplicity of solutions to the problem \eqref{LS} in alternative variational frameworks, depending on the range of the parameter $β.$ We do not rely on bifurcation or degree theory, which have been used in the literature for logistic-type problems. Instead, the novelty is to obtain min-max type solutions by exploiting the different geometry of the functional associated with the logistic problem. In case $N\geq 2$, we establish existence and multiplicity results for different ranges of the coupling parameter, considering both the case \(2<p<4\) and the classical case \(p=4\). We also investigate the asymptotic behavior of selected solutions as \(β\to1^-\) and \(β\to-\infty\).}
  \textsc{Key words: Logistic System, Ground State Solution, Linking structure, Seminodal Solution.}{\small}
  \textsc{MSC 2020: } {\small 35J15 35J20, 35J60, 62J12.}

In this paper, we study the Schrödinger-Maxwell equation with critical growth and mass subcritical perturbation
  \begin{align*}
  \begin{cases}
  \displaystyle - Δu+λu+ \left(|x|^{-1}\ast| u|^2\right) u
  =|u|^{4}u +|u|^{p-2}u\quad\quad \text{in} \ \mathbb{ R}^3,
  \displaystyle \int_{\mathbb{R}^3}|u|^2dx=a^2,
  \end{cases}
  \end{align*} where $a>0$ is prescribed, $3<p<\frac{10}{3}$, $\ast$ denotes the convolution and $λ\in \mathbb{R}$ appears as a Lagrange multiplier. When the nonlocal convolution term is absent, the above equation can be reduced to the Schrödinger equation. In this case, the previous works showed that it admits a local minimizer (ground-state) and a mountain-pass type solution. By contrast, we surprisingly find that the above equation has no solutions with negative energy provided $a>0$ is sufficiently small. More precisely, we rigorously prove that the above equation possesses a mountain-pass type solution with positive energy for sufficiently small $a>0$, which reveals an essential difference in solution structure between the Schrödinger-Maxwell equation and the classical Schrödinger equation in the parameter range $3<p<\frac{10}{3}$. The main difficulty encountered in this work is the loss of compactness arising from the Sobolev critical term. Furthermore, we explore the dynamical properties of the corresponding time-dependent problem and establish the strong instability of its standing wave solutions. It is worth pointing out that this work refines and extends the existing literature by covering the unexplored parameter regime $p\in(3,\frac{10}{3})$.

We prove a local higher integrability result for the gradient of \textit{a priori} Hölder continuous weak solutions to the parabolic double phase equation \[ \partial_t u - \operatorname{div} \left(|Du|^{p-2}Du + a(z)|Du|^{q-2}Du\right)
  = 0 \qquad \text{in } Ω_T. \] We work under a relaxed gap condition on the exponents $p$ and $q$. The coefficient $a$ is assumed to belong to the class $\mathcal{Z}^κ(Ω_T)$ for some $κ\in (0,\infty)$. The functions in this class satisfy a one-sided pointwise bound that controls how fast $a$ can grow away from its zero set, and the class contains the Hölder continuous functions. We also impose a \emph{mild almost increasing} condition on $a$, which motivates the introduction of a new mollification, which we call the \emph{slanted Steklov average}. For $u \in C^{0,γ,γ/q}_{\mathrm{loc}}(Ω_T)$ with $γ\in [0,1)$, our main result holds under the gap bound \begin{equation}\tag{G}\label{eq:G}
  2 \le p \le q, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, (q-p)(q-2γ)\leq qκ\end{equation} The new gap condition \eqref{eq:G} is purely parabolic in nature and is stricter than the optimal gap relation associated with the Lavrentiev phenomenon for the elliptic double phase functional.

Functional Analysis.summary:
Let \(0<p,q<\infty\). We prove \[
  L_q(0,1)\hookrightarrow\mathcal C_{p,q}
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q=2. \] As a consequence, we obtain, for all \(0<p_1,q_1,p_2,q_2<\infty\), \[
  L_{p_1,q_1}(0,1)\hookrightarrow\mathcal C_{p_2,q_2}
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p_1=q_1=2. \]
